// `let c = c` is one mistake, and used to be reported as two.
//
// The initialiser is resolved before the name is declared, so `c` on the right
// is genuinely undefined — that is the RS001, and it is right. But `use`
// declares the name as a recovery binding so the same typo is not reported at
// every later use, and `declare` then mistook that for a real declaration and
// added a false RS004 claiming `c` was declared twice — with a note citing the
// line of the *use* as "the earlier one". Across lines that note named a line
// which declares nothing at all.
//
// A recovery binding is not a declaration. The same two kinds are already
// paired this way where assignment checks mutability.
let c = c
print(c)
